## Why This Is Asked

This question tests real-world patterns: per-field state via closures, per-field debouncing, cancellation of outdated requests with AbortController, and a global concurrency limiter to prevent overload, plus mapping responses to correct field.

## Key Concepts

- Closures for per-field state
- Debounce patterns
- AbortController cancellation
- Concurrency limit with a simple semaphore
- Mapping async results to UI per-field

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal concurrency-limited debounced fetch per field using closures
function createFieldController(fetchUrl, maxConcurrent = 4) {
  let debounceTimer;
  let abortController;

  return {
    onInput: async (term) => {
      // Cancel previous request
      if (abortController) {
        abortController.abort();
      }

      // Clear existing debounce
      clearTimeout(debounceTimer);

      // Set new debounce
      debounceTimer = setTimeout(async () => {
        // Check global concurrency limit
        if (globalInFlight >= maxConcurrent) {
          await waitForSlot();
        }

        globalInFlight++;
        abortController = new AbortController();

        try {
          const response = await fetch(`${fetchUrl}?q=${term}`, {
            signal: abortController.signal
          });

          if (!abortController.signal.aborted) {
            const suggestions = await response.json();
            updateFieldUI(suggestions);
          }
        } catch (error) {
          if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
            console.error('Fetch error:', error);
          }
        } finally {
          globalInFlight--;
        }
      }, 300);
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures and promises in a realistic data loader, including deduplicating in-flight requests and time-based invalidation.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and encapsulation
- Promise deduplication for identical requests
- Mutable in-memory cache with TTL
- Edge cases: error handling, cache busting

## Code Example
```javascript
function makeCachedFetcher(fetchFn) {
  const TTL = 2 * 60 * 1000;
  const cache = new Map();
  return async function load(id) {
    const entry = cache.get(id);
    const now = Date.now();
    if (entry?.value !== undefined && (now - entry.time) < TTL) {
      return entry.value;
    }
    if (entry?.promise) {
      return entry.promise;
    }
    const p = fetchFn(id).then(res => {
      cache.set(id, { value: res, time: Date.now() });
      return res;
    }).catch(err => {
      cache.delete(id);
      throw err;
    });
    cache.set(id, { promise: p });
    return p;
  };
}

// Usage
const fakeFetch = async (id) => ({ id, value: 'val-'+id });
const loadUser = makeCachedFetcher(fakeFetch);
loadUser(1).then(console.log); // { id: 1, value: 'val-1' }
```

